> Making Onigiri is rather straightforward. Thus, this recipe focuses on varieties of fillings rather than the basic process. If you need a refresher or more details, see:  https://www.justonecookbook.com/onigiri-rice-balls/

== Basic Preparation (Refresher) ==

Prepare @rice{}(white, usually calrose) according to rice cooker instructions. Optionally add a small amount of flavoring to rice while it cooks (sesame oil, rice vinegar, etc.)

> 3 rice cooker cups of uncooked Japanese short-grain rice yield 6⅔ cups (990 g) of cooked white rice. This is enough for 9 onigiri rice balls. Adjust the amount of rice you make according to your desired servings.

Spread rice out onto a plate and let cool until it's just warm to the touch.

Spritz some rice vinegar onto the rice if you didn't add during the rice making step

Using molding method of your choice, add your filling.

Sprinkle on your @furikake{}.

Roast @seaweed{} using an open flame and then wrap onigiri in your desired style.

> If you'd like to pack Onigiri for lunch, keep it cool and consume within 6 hours. Onigiri (or any rice dish) gets hard when you refrigerate it. The cold air makes the rice dry and hard. I don't recommend making onigiri too far ahead of time. But if you really need to, my trick is to wrap the onigiri with thick kitchen towels and store it in the fridge. The onigiri will be cool and safe but should not get cold.

> Another option, if your onigiri has dried out a bit with storage, is to grill it in a frying pan and baste it with soy sauce. This is called Yaki Onigiri.

== Salted Salmon ==

Combine @salmon{1%can}(cut into flakes) and @salt{}(to taste).

== Okaka ==

Combine @katsuobush{1%3-5 oz pkg}(dried bonito flakes) with @soy sauce{1%tbsp}. This should moisten but not drench the bonito flakes.

== Spicy Salmon-Mayo ==

Combine @salmon{1%can}(cut into flakes), @kewpie mayo{3%tbsp}, @sriracha{2%tbsp}, @?eel sauce of Japanese BBQ sauce{2%tbsp}, @soy sauce{1%tbsp}, @pepper{}(to taste), and @salt{}(to taste).

== Tuna Mayo ==

Combine @tuna{1%can}(preferably packed in olive oil, cut into flakes), @kewpie mayo{2%tbsp}, and @soy sauce{1/2%tbsp}.

== Spicy Tuna Mayo ==

Combine @tuna{1%can}(preferably packed in olive oil, cut into flakes), @kewpie mayo{2%tbsp}, @soy sauce{1/2%tbsp}, @shichimi togarashi{}(to taste), and @?sriracha{2%tbsp}.

== Miscellaneous Other Fillings ==

Try things like @?umeboshi{2}(Japanese pickled plum, deseeded), @?shio kombu{}, or @?furikake{}.